{#
    The tab icon, for every page that has a <head> of its own.

    Included rather than repeated because a page that omits it does not merely
    look unbranded: the browser falls back to requesting /favicon.ico, which
    matches no route, and Symfony logs the 404 at error level. The admin log
    filled with "No route found for GET /favicon.ico" every time someone opened
    "show original", which is a real fault report about nothing.

    This used to be an emoji in a data URI — ✉️ — which cost no request but was
    not the product's mark, and rendered as whatever envelope the reader's
    operating system happens to draw. A tab strip is where people find plMail
    among thirty other tabs, and the thing they look for is the icon.

    Now the real mark, as an SVG that stays sharp at 16px and any pixel ratio.

    It is the ONLY tab icon linked, and that is deliberate. This used to be
    followed by `<link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="192x192"
    href="/icons/icon-192.png">` as a fallback, and the fallback won: Firefox
    stores an SVG icon at width 65535 and a PNG at its declared size, and picks
    the smallest icon that is big enough for the tab, which is the 192px PNG.
    A static file can only wear the product default, so every account saw the
    wax seal in its tabs whatever icon it had chosen. A second candidate can
    only be wrong for someone. The PNGs still serve the places that need a
    raster and have no per-account route: the apple-touch-icon in app.html.twig
    and the manifest's install icons.

    The SVG comes from a route rather than a file, because the logo is a
    per-user setting now (Settings → Appearance → Logo: an icon, then its
    paint) and a static file can only wear one. Anonymous pages get the product
    default from the same route; /icons/favicon.svg still exists, painted in
    that default, for anything that fetches icons without a page.
#}
{# The choice is the URL — /branding/favicon/<icon>/<paint>.svg — and the
   route draws what the URL says, never what the session says. Browsers keep a
   favicon cache of their own that ignores ordinary revalidation until a hard
   reload, so a new choice has to be a new URL, and the appearance page swaps
   it live on every pick (see repaintLogo) — before the save has landed, which
   is why the drawing cannot come from the session. See BrandingController. #}
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{ logo_favicon_url(appearance().effectiveLogoMotif, appearance().effectiveLogoPaint) }}">
